s StaticNested {  // Valid static nested class\n        static void staticMethod() {}  // Static method\n        void instanceMethod() {}       // Non-static method (allowed!)\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **Existence of `static class`**  \n   Java supports `static` **only for nested classes** (inside another class). Top-level classes cannot be static.\n\n2. **Meaning**  \n   A `static` nested class:\n   - Doesn't require an instance of the outer class for instantiation\n   - Has **no implicit access to outer class instance members** (can access outer's `static` members)\n   - Behaves like a top-level class but is nested for packaging/logical grouping\n\n3. **Must methods be static?**  \n   **No.** Static nested classes can have both:\n   - `static` methods (call via `StaticNestedClass.staticMethod()`)\n   - Instance methods (call via `new StaticNestedClass().instanceMethod()`)\n\n4. **Reverse requirement?**  \n   If a nested class contains **only static methods**, the class **must be declared static**.  \n   *Reason*: Non-static nested classes (inner classes) **cannot** declare static members (except `static final` constants). This is enforced by Java's compiler.\n\n   *Exception*: Top-level classes (non-nested) are implicitly \"static\" in behavior and **don't** need/allow a `static` modifier, even with all-static methods.\n\n5. **Use Cases**  \n   Static nested classes are ideal for:\n   - **Utility groupings**: Bundle related helpers (e.g., `MathUtils` inside `Calculator`)\n   - **Builder Pattern**: Stateless builders (e.g., `OuterClass.Builder`)\n   - **Reducing namespaces**: Avoid top-level class proliferation\n   - **Encapsulation**: Limit visibility to outer class only\n   - **Performance**: Lighter than inner classes (no hidden outer-class reference)\n\n<answer>\n**Summary**  \n- `static class` exists **only for nested classes** (not top-level classes).  \n- Methods **don't need to be static**; static nested classes support both static and instance me